  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA dynamic system will track from the Midwest and into the Great\nLakes over the next 24 hours. Storms are currently producing \ndamaging wind gusts and heavy rain in central Illinois will \ncontinue to advance east. By sunset, the cluster of \nstorms and showers will likely be near western Ohio. Given \nsufficient wind just above the surface (50-65kts at 850mb), \nthere will likely be a pre-frontal rain shield. High-resolution \nsoundings indicate a boundary layer inversion between 7pm and \n2am, which may inhibit surface gusts until the front arrives.The\nprimary window for damaging wind gusts, tied to the frontal \npassage, is expected between 2am and 7am Thursday in eastern \nOhio and western Pennsylvania. At the moment, the Storm\nPrediction Center continues to hold a Slight and Marginal in\nportions for this noted threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nConditions will dry out from west to east early Thursday morning\nbehind a cold front, though it will remain windy. Even as rain\nchances decrease, expect gusty conditions to persist throughout\nthe day due to diurnal mixing of strong winds aloft. Wind gusts\nof 40-45mph are possible by early afternoon, with higher gusts\nin eastern Tucker County. Probability of 50mph gusts in the wake\nof the front is above 70% in the ridges of West Virginia. \nTherefore, a Wind Advisory is in effect from 7am to 6pm Thursday\nfor eastern Tucker. Winds should subside as the storm system \nmoves away later in the evening. \n\nDepending on the precise positioning of the cold front relative\nto the Laurel Highlands and northern West Virginia Thursday\nafternoon, a few isolated storms could develop and produce\ndamaging wind gusts. The Storm Prediction Center has outlined\nthis region under Marginal Risk for severe thunderstorms. The\nprobability of thunderstorms rapidly decreases after 4pm\nThursday. \n\nDry weather returns Friday and Saturday as high pressure builds\nacross the region. More widespread rain chances return Sunday\ninto Monday as low pressure tracks across the region.",
